{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S39xdpXaQPiEZ2cRi2WXFS","type":"tool_result","content":"/home/micha/.nvm/versions/node/v24.22.1/bin/node\n/home/linuxbrew/.linuxbrew/bin/python3\n/usr/bin/gh\n/home/linuxbrew/.linuxbrew/bin/curl\n/home/linuxbrew/.linuxbrew/bin/git\nv24.22.1\ntmpfs           9.8G  7.6G  2.3G  78% /tmp\n/dev/sdd       1007G  116G  841G  13% /\n4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72 A builder's copy of the engine is theirs to change (v0.85.2) (#202)\n\nThe owner's word: an agent that clones the repository (or takes the handoff package or a single file) to build a game may change the engine in any way it sees fit (D76). The manuals said the opposite: the API card's and the guide's \"Never edit the engine.\" (the v0.85.0 audit's wording, never a decision), the single files' headers (\"Do not change it unless you are asked to extend the engine itself\"; \"keep everything outside GAME START and GAME END as it is\"), and the agent edition's and the 3D engine's manuals (read the code \"only to debug\"). Each now says the engine is the builder's to change and where a readable copy is; README.md and CLAUDE.md say the repository's rules are for what merges into main, not for a clone kept for a game of one's own; DOCTRINE.md says how a builder's copy sits with the principles; doctrine-check's part 7 holds the seven manuals to it.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17695eeb-ca5d-4805-90b6-169496893b17","uuid":"1862473a-aa36-4c4c-94bf-e239fefde9b9","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:21:57.471Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"/home/micha/.nvm/versions/node/v24.22.1/bin/node\n/home/linuxbrew/.linuxbrew/bin/python3\n/usr/bin/gh\n/home/linuxbrew/.linuxbrew/bin/curl\n/home/linuxbrew/.linuxbrew/bin/git\nv24.22.1\ntmpfs           9.8G  7.6G  2.3G  78% /tmp\n/dev/sdd       1007G  116G  841G  13% /\n4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72 A builder's copy of the engine is theirs to change (v0.85.2) (#202)\n\nThe owner's word: an agent that clones the repository (or takes the handoff package or a single file) to build a game may change the engine in any way it sees fit (D76). The manuals said the opposite: the API card's and the guide's \"Never edit the engine.\" (the v0.85.0 audit's wording, never a decision), the single files' headers (\"Do not change it unless you are asked to extend the engine itself\"; \"keep everything outside GAME START and GAME END as it is\"), and the agent edition's and the 3D engine's manuals (read the code \"only to debug\"). Each now says the engine is the builder's to change and where a readable copy is; README.md and CLAUDE.md say the repository's rules are for what merges into main, not for a clone kept for a game of one's own; DOCTRINE.md says how a builder's copy sits with the principles; doctrine-check's part 7 holds the seven manuals to it.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[],"moreFiles":0,"unavailable":true}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01S39xdpXaQPiEZ2cRi2WXFS","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
