{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi9cJBGJUbCw4V6v72Jf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cricket Kerfuffle is finished: everything in TASK.md is implemented, and the full test command passes (453 checks in 20 suites, including the browser smoke runs and the AI Hub harness with 1, 2 and 4 players).\n\nOn the hook's two points:\n- **The About check:** it was already fixed before my earlier final run (432 passed, 0 failed). After this round's changes I ran the full command again (453 passed), then made two last text edits (the issue counts and the session time). The quick suites that cover the About pages and exit interview pass after those.\n- **The four items I'd listed as simplified are now built and tested** (new `sim-extras` suite, plus screenshots):\n  - **Crowd catches on sixes:** a six that comes down in the stands, or among the deck chairs just past a village or beach rope, is often caught by a spectator, who holds the ball up. It gets a CROWD CATCH! callout, a cheer and its own commentary, and the attract showreel includes one. Forced sixes now land just past the rope; before, they flew about twice the rope's distance.\n  - **Seagulls:** gulls circle over the outfield. When one steals a ball at rest, it dives, carries the ball off and drops it 30 units away for 2 byes.\n  - **Fog:** a ball more than 260 units from the batter fades out unless it's lit (aflame, holy light or steam). Distant fielders fade too.\n  - **The Nets:** two netted lanes. The one you play in has back and side nets round the batter that stop the ball and stay open in front; the keeper stands inside the net. The second lane has its own stumps and bowling machine.\n- **Two small fixes found along the way:** callouts that fire together now stack instead of overprinting, and no \"single\" commentary plays for a ball going for six.\n\nThe game is `CricketKerfuffle-Cricket-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-062125.html`, beside TASK.md with `exit-interview.json` (it parses, every field answered). Dev files, tests (`node test/run.mjs`) and the engine change log are in the `CricketKerfuffle-Cricket-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-062125/` subfolder. No server I started is still running.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 06:21:25\n- **Source:** Cricket (over-the-top arcade style)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade sports (cricket) with a comedy story campaign. 2.5D projected 3D: a telephoto TV broadcast camera behind the bowler for every delivery, a high wide camera following the ball, a side-on review camera and a close-up camera; three-quarter view for the story scenes.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, modified: see engine/CHANGES-KERFUFFLE.md), AI Hub Session Protocol v1 client 1.4.0, plain JavaScript, Web Audio; tests on Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's HD Humanoid pixel style at 320x240 expanded, canvas lighting, with new cricket assets (a KERFUFFLE shape set of 40 objects and 42 materials: stumps, pavilion, pub, greenhouse, church, Ferris wheel, jumbotron, the Thrumptonator, Doris the cow and more), five grounds painted from recipes and far-end panoramas\n- **Animation:** The engine's procedural Humanoid rigs driven by cricket attack specs (vertical-plane strokes, a windmill bowling arm), poses, a solvePose wrapper for umpire signals, and a two-handed bat grip added to the engine\n- **Audio:** All code: chip-synth music (10 original songs with Hype and ON FIRE layers, 11 stings), synthesized SFX, a crowd engine (murmur, applause, formant roars and a KER-FUF-FLE chant) and Animalese-style gibberish formant voices\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, parallel mode: a Super Over Showdown for 1-4 players with the game's own card; local multiplayer for 1-4 on one screen\n- **Testing:** Headless engine (game.advance, game.debug) in Node vm contexts, scripted playthroughs and bot matches, Playwright browser checks and screenshots, the AI Hub harness\n- **Research:** None beyond the provided materials: the plan, the engine handoff docs and kits, and the AI Hub README and harness\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** Session time about 2 h 30 min by the system clock (06:21 to about 08:50); tokens and cost unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 36 issues across 20 suites (453 checks) and the screenshot reviews: a delivery solver off by up to 3 units, a catch ring judged 20 ms before the catch, a run counted when both batters were sent back, a keeper also picked to bowl, a held advert or tea-tray pause that never counted down, Doris the cow shooing fielders off a ball resting by her, a result that ignored the Kerfuffle Method's revised target, throws from behind the stumps scored as missed shies, the toss screen crashing, a jumbotron covering tall screens, forced sixes hit twice the rope's distance, callouts drawn over each other, and more.\n2. **How many would be considered critical / game breaking?** About 8 were game-breaking: the two stalls (the pause that never ended and the cow), the keeper bowling to himself, runs counted for a batter sent back, the rain-rule result, the uncatchable catch ring, the toss crash, and the ball leaving the bowler's hand mid run-up.\n3. **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The bot-campaign suite: the whole story played by the autopilot, chapter by chapter with retries. It found the Kerfuffle Method result bug, the difficulty multipliers handicapping the player's own autopilot, non-deterministic match seeds, and a chapter too steep to win.\n4. **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The resolver-table rows in unit-shots: they restate the data table back to itself and never failed.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about the engine?** The Humanoid rig and its attack specs: vertical-plane swings gave straight-bat strokes and the bowling windmill without hand-made animation. Close behind: shapes as data with asset-sheet's contact sheets, and exact headless runs with game.advance.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes, a few times: game.go clears timers, so a deep link scheduled before the first scene never fired; palm and cactus are props, not shapes; the pixel font lacks the middle dot and ellipsis; and since draw doesn't run headless, mouse layouts computed only while drawing were missing in tests.\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The cricket simulation itself: ball physics with an exact predictor, the shot resolver and timing model, fielders' interception, catches and throws, running between wickets, LBW truth with Owl-Eye and the review system, plus the TV camera director and the painted far-end panorama.\n4. **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Reading depth in the low orthographic broadcast camera: a ball coming at the viewer has no perspective, so the shadow, pitch marker and trail have to carry it. A telephoto lens's far stands had to be faked with a painted panorama, and objects behind the camera culled by hand.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?** Six changes, logged in engine/CHANGES-KERFUFFLE.md: (1) a cricket bat weapon with a two-handed grip, because the rig only had swords, guns and staffs; (2) pads, brim hats, helmet grilles, keeper gloves, moustaches and spectacles, for cricket kit and characters; (3) a voice bus and Voices volume, so the gibberish voices and crowd follow the player's volumes; (4) PlayStation button symbols by default, Xbox the option, as TASK.md asks; (5) Create + Options left to the AI Hub; (6) ten floor bakes kept instead of six, so the match cameras never re-bake.\n6. **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** A perspective (telephoto) ground camera, or near-plane culling for the orthographic views: every sports or racing game wants the camera behind the action looking down a long axis.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about this prompt?** The definition of done: a feature-complete vertical slice, a scripted full playthrough, an attract mode and dev mode. Those requirements forced the game to be robust, not just a demo of one match.\n2. **What was confusing?** How far the plan binds: its folder names carried another model's suffix, and some of its numbers (a 30-run chase, fielders at 15 m) needed retuning once played. TASK.md winning settled the names, not the numbers. And Online links only the hub, while multiplayer must also work for the game on its own.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** Scope: five grounds, six teams, a five-chapter story, eleven lessons, AI Hub sessions, local multiplayer, an attract showreel and dev mode, all complete with no stubs, plus making cricket's depth readable in an orthographic camera.\n4. **What would you change in this prompt?** Give a priority order for when the plan's content outruns the time, say the plan's numbers are starting values to tune, and supply token and cost figures so the Usage field can be filled."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1025,"cache_read_input_tokens":906183,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1025},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17695eeb-ca5d-4805-90b6-169496893b17","uuid":"e9a9f53c-eeae-451c-98a1-8d1755843fe0","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:47:33.269Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi9cHfkyB7pwQs73qYxj"}
